#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Tell the session when GNOME Shell has finished starting, so the streaming
server can be started after it rather than before it.

    hdw4s-stage-wait [grace-seconds [bound-seconds]]

Prints, on stdout, one line at a time:

    armed            its event selections are in place on the X server
    shown <ms> id=<window> parent=<window> name=<title>
                     the compositor's stage window was mapped, <ms> after
                     armed; the ids say WHICH window it was
    lost <ms> id=<window>
                     that stage window was destroyed (the shell restarted);
                     a new "shown" follows if a new stage appears
    ready <ms> shown=<ms>
                     the shell set the stage's input region, which it does
                     only once its startup has finished
    bound <ms> shown=<ms>
                     <bound-seconds> passed after "shown" with no "ready"
    none <reason>    there is nothing this can watch for: a window manager
                     with no clutter stage, or an X server without SHAPE

and exits after ready, bound or none. Anything else -- no output, an
exception, the X server going away -- is for the caller to treat as "do not
wait", which is why this prints nothing it is not sure of. hdw4s-run-session
waits on it with deadlines of its own and kills it afterwards.

WHY THIS EXISTS: two GNOME 46 defects, both of which leave a starting desktop
black for good with every process healthy and idle.

  1. mutter 46: the stage's first frame is discarded while the stage is not
     yet shown, that discard never clears the flag saying an update is already
     scheduled, and one input event queued before the stage is shown then
     makes clutter refuse every later update. The streaming server injects the
     visitor's pointer as soon as a browser connects. Fixed in mutter 47.3
     (GNOME issue 3804, commit 24083e1e), never backported to 46.
  2. gnome-shell 46: between the stage being shown and the end of startup, the
     shell waits for its backgrounds to load with the whole interface hidden.
     A screen size change in that span destroys what it is waiting for, the
     wait never ends, and the interface stays hidden over a flat background.
     The streaming server resizes the screen to the browser as soon as one
     connects. Fixed in gnome-shell 50 (GNOME issue 9018), not in 46 to 49.

So the stream must start neither before the stage is shown (1) nor between
that and the end of startup (2). Holding it until "shown" alone made (2)
MORE likely, by moving the browser's resize into exactly that span.

WHAT MARKS THE END OF STARTUP. gnome-shell sets the stage window's input
shape only from a routine that does nothing until startup has finished and no
modal dialog is up. So the first ShapeInput change on the stage AFTER it maps
comes after startup-complete, never before it (measured: 0.4 s after idle,
0.6-0.8 s after under load). mutter also clears that shape once, when the
stage is created, which is BEFORE the map and is ignored here. The signal can
be LATE or ABSENT -- a shell that starts into the overview, or with a dialog
up, sets no input shape at startup -- and that is what "bound" is for: once
the stage has been shown for long enough that startup must have finished, the
wait ends without the signal. It says NOTHING about whether the desktop is
healthy; a black desktop is released by "bound" too. Do not turn this into a
health check.

HOW THE STAGE IS RECOGNISED, without its name. It is the only window whose
parent is neither the root nor a child of the root: the overlay window is
never listed among the root's children, while every decoration frame is. The
title is not used because it is the process name taken from argv[0], and any
client, including a framed one, can carry any title. The overlay window
itself is never requested: asking the server for it maps it. Once one window
has been taken as the stage, no other window counts until it is destroyed.

WHEN THERE IS NO STAGE. A desktop whose window manager is not a clutter
compositor (xfwm4, metacity, a picom setup) never reparents anything into the
overlay. Once a window manager has announced itself on the root
(_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK) and no reparent into the overlay has followed
within the grace period, this says so and exits, so such a desktop does not
wait out the caller's deadline on every start. This is decided by what runs,
not by the session's name or desktop label. One known false match, harmless:
an XEmbed tray icon also moves from the root into a parent that is not a root
child, but under GNOME nothing embeds before the stage is shown, and anywhere
else the answer it produces -- do not wait -- is the right one anyway.

HDW4S_STAGE_WAIT_DEBUG=1 adds "debug <what> <ms>" lines on stderr.

REMOVE THIS when Ubuntu 24.04 leaves hdw4s's supported set, or when the
supported releases carry BOTH fixes above and the reproducer
(private/reproduce-motion-deadlock.sh, a motion arm and a resize arm) no
longer goes black with HDW4S_STARTUP_HOLD=off. With only one of the two
fixes, this stays.

python3 -I, so a PYTHONPATH or a user site-packages directory cannot replace
the X library this reads with. Nothing but the standard library is imported
at module level, and the arguments are read inside main(): an exception out
here would reach Ubuntu's crash handler, which takes seconds to give up, and
those seconds would be spent from the caller's wait. It creates no window,
takes no grab, sends no input, and changes no property.
"""

import os
import select
import sys
import time

BACKSTOP = 600.0   # never outlive a caller that forgot us


def say(line):
    sys.stdout.write(line + "\n")
    sys.stdout.flush()


def main():
    grace = float(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 3.0
    bound = float(sys.argv[2]) if len(sys.argv) > 2 else 18.0

    from Xlib import X, display, error

    dpy = display.Display()
    root = dpy.screen().root
    wm_check = dpy.intern_atom("_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK")
    debug = os.environ.get("HDW4S_STAGE_WAIT_DEBUG") == "1"

    # Checked before "armed", so the caller never waits for a signal that
    # cannot come. Releasing at "shown" instead would be the hold that made
    # the second defect more likely, so without SHAPE there is no hold.
    if not dpy.has_extension("SHAPE"):
        sys.stderr.write("hdw4s-stage-wait: the X server has no SHAPE extension\n")
        say("none no SHAPE extension, so the end of startup cannot be seen")
        return

    def root_children():
        return {w.id for w in root.query_tree().children}

    def watch(win):
        try:
            win.change_attributes(event_mask=X.StructureNotifyMask)
        except error.XError:
            pass

    root.change_attributes(event_mask=X.SubstructureNotifyMask | X.PropertyChangeMask)
    for w in root.query_tree().children:
        watch(w)
    dpy.sync()
    armed = time.monotonic()
    say("armed")

    def ms(t=None):
        return int(((time.monotonic() if t is None else t) - armed) * 1000)

    def note(what):
        if debug:
            sys.stderr.write("debug %s %d\n" % (what, ms()))
            sys.stderr.flush()

    def describe(win):
        try:
            parent = win.query_tree().parent.id
            prop = win.get_property(dpy.intern_atom("_NET_WM_NAME"),
                                    X.AnyPropertyType, 0, 16)
            name = prop.value.decode("utf-8", "replace") if prop else ""
        except error.XError:
            parent, name = 0, ""
        return "id=%#x parent=%#x name=%s" % (win.id, parent,
                                              name.replace(" ", "_") or "-")

    def in_overlay(win):
        """Parented where only the stage lives, and whether it is mapped."""
        try:
            parent = win.query_tree().parent.id
            mapped = win.get_attributes().map_state != X.IsUnmapped
        except error.XError:
            return False, False
        return parent != root.id and parent not in root_children(), mapped

    stage = None       # the stage window's id, once one has been seen
    ever_staged = False
    shown_at = None    # monotonic time the current stage was mapped

    def adopt(win):
        """Take win as the stage. The input-shape selection is made here,
        before the map, so a change cannot slip in ahead of it."""
        nonlocal stage, ever_staged
        stage, ever_staged = win.id, True
        watch(win)
        try:
            win.shape_select_input(1)
        except error.XError:
            pass
        note("stage")

    def shown(win):
        nonlocal shown_at
        shown_at = time.monotonic()
        say("shown %d %s" % (ms(shown_at), describe(win)))

    def done(line):
        say(line)
        dpy.close()
        sys.exit(0)

    wm_seen = root.get_full_property(wm_check, X.AnyPropertyType, sizehint=1) is not None
    wm_since = armed if wm_seen else None
    if wm_seen:
        note("wm")
    fd = dpy.fileno()
    while time.monotonic() - armed < BACKSTOP:
        now = time.monotonic()
        if wm_since is not None and not ever_staged and now - wm_since > grace:
            done("none window manager without a compositor stage")
        if shown_at is not None and now - shown_at >= bound:
            done("bound %d shown=%d" % (ms(now), ms(shown_at)))
        while dpy.pending_events():
            ev = dpy.next_event()
            if ev.type == X.CreateNotify:
                watch(ev.window)
            elif ev.type == X.ReparentNotify:
                # The stage moving into the overlay. Checking map_state closes
                # the race where it was already mapped by the time we look.
                if stage is None:
                    inside, mapped = in_overlay(ev.window)
                    if inside:
                        adopt(ev.window)
                        if mapped:
                            shown(ev.window)
            elif ev.type == X.MapNotify:
                if stage is None:
                    inside, _ = in_overlay(ev.window)
                    if inside:
                        adopt(ev.window)
                if ev.window.id == stage and shown_at is None:
                    shown(ev.window)
            elif ev.type == X.DestroyNotify and ev.window.id == stage:
                # A new shell makes a new stage, with its own startup ahead
                # of it: the old one's bound must not release the stream
                # inside the new one's.
                sys.stderr.write("hdw4s-stage-wait: the stage window was destroyed\n")
                say("lost %d id=%#x" % (ms(), stage))
                stage, shown_at = None, None
            elif ev.type == dpy.extension_event.ShapeNotify:
                # python3-xlib 0.33 (Ubuntu 24.04) calls the window
                # "affected_window"; reading "window" raised and ended the
                # watcher on every start that reached this line.
                win = getattr(ev, "affected_window", None) or getattr(ev, "window", None)
                if win is None or win.id != stage:
                    continue
                note("shape-k%d" % ev.shape_kind)
                if ev.shape_kind == 2 and shown_at is not None:
                    done("ready %d shown=%d" % (ms(), ms(shown_at)))
            elif ev.type == X.PropertyNotify and ev.atom == wm_check:
                if wm_since is None:
                    wm_since = time.monotonic()
                    note("wm")
        dpy.flush()
        wait = 0.2
        if shown_at is not None:
            wait = max(0.0, min(wait, shown_at + bound - time.monotonic()))
        select.select([fd], [], [], wait)
    sys.exit(1)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        main()
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except BaseException as e:   # noqa: BLE001 -- silence is the fail-open signal
        sys.stderr.write("hdw4s-stage-wait: %s: %s\n" % (type(e).__name__, e))
        sys.exit(1)
